  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nThe Winter Storm System continues to unfold across the area as \nexpected with a tight transition line from snow to rain right along \nthe Mississippi River. No changes were made to the headlines\nthis morning. Snowfall reports this morning have been in the 2 \nto 6 inch range, heaviest toward northern Bollinger into Perry \nCounty in Missouri. Meanwhile rainfall totals east of the \nMississippi are approaching an inch or a little bit better. \nRadar shows a wide swath of precipitation streaming into the \nQuad State from the southeast early this morning as the upper \nlow drifts slowly across northern AR toward northern \nMississippi. \n\nMoisture will continue wrapping around the low and will even be \nenhanced as moisture transport increases as flow off the Atlantic \npicks up this morning with a deepening low off the eastern seaboard. \n850-700mb FGEN banding along with a surface trough draped across the \narea will continue to provide forcing for ascent throughout the day \ninto this evening before gradually diminishing. The thermal profile \ncontinues to show support for snow west of the Mississippi River \nthis morning; however, the lowest 5000ft of the atmosphere is \nexpected to gradually warm above freezing by noon or shortly \nthereafter. That will allow the precipitation to transition over to \nall rain. Any travel issues should quickly improve as temperatures \nare expected to be just above freezing. Again, east of the \nMississippi, thermal profiles continue to support rain.  Additional \nrainfall totals are expected to be between 1 and 2 inches with \nadditional snow expected to be in the 1 to 3 inch range over \nsoutheast Missouri.\n\nDrier air is set to arrive as the aforementioned features shift east \nof the Quad State, bringing gradually clearing skies and \ntemperatures warming into the 50s by Saturday.\n\nThe next chance of precipitation will arrive Saturday night into \nSunday as lee side cyclogenesis occurs with a trough digging across \nthe Plains. The low is set to wrap up from Colorado into western \nOklahoma. Mid level lapse rates are set to steepen to around 6-7C/km \nahead of the low and associated cold front as it passes directly \nover the Quad State. That should be enough to result in thunderstorm \ndevelopment. Overall instability isn't too impressive with limited \nmoisture return into the area with the very quick movement of the \nsystem. In fact, dewpoints are only forecast to be in the 45-50 \ndegree range. Otherwise, temperatures are expected to warm into the \nupper 50s for Sunday.\n\nDry high pressure will build across the area at the surface with \nridging aloft. That will lead to near to above normal temperatures \nand no rain or snow for much of next week.",
